'en_content'=><<<'HTML'
<p>An air fryer does not fry food by submerging it in oil. It behaves more like a compact, powerful convection oven, circulating hot air around the food. That creates an obvious question: if the appliance is designed to cook with less fat, <strong>does using extra virgin olive oil make sense?</strong> Yes. In many recipes a small amount of EVOO improves browning, texture and flavour without turning the process into deep frying.</p>
<p>The aim is not to fill the basket with oil. It is to <strong>coat the food with a very thin film</strong>. Amount, temperature and distribution matter much more than volume.</p>
<h2>Can EVOO handle air-fryer temperatures?</h2>
<p>Under normal domestic use, yes. Air fryers operate in temperature ranges that overlap with conventional roasting and frying. The International Olive Council highlights olive oil's heat stability, linked to its high proportion of oleic acid and its natural antioxidants, and uses about 180°C as a typical ideal temperature for many traditional frying applications.</p>
<p>That does not mean EVOO should always be pushed to the hottest setting. An air fryer may reach 200°C or more, depending on the model, while many foods cook well between 160 and 190°C. <strong>Use the temperature the food actually needs rather than defaulting to maximum heat.</strong></p>
<h2>How much EVOO should you use?</h2>
<p>There is no universal quantity because food volume, surface area and natural fat content vary. For a domestic batch of vegetables, potatoes or a lean cut of meat, <strong>one or two teaspoons spread evenly</strong> may be enough. Some recipes need a little more, while naturally fatty foods may need none.</p>
<p>The best guide is the surface: aim for a light coating rather than oil collecting in the bottom. If the food is soaked in oil, you are probably using more than this cooking method requires.</p>
<h2>The best ways to apply it</h2>
<ul>
<li><strong>Bowl and toss:</strong> add a small amount of EVOO to potatoes or vegetables and mix before transferring them to the basket.</li>
<li><strong>Brush:</strong> useful for meat, vegetable slices and delicate foods.</li>
<li><strong>Refillable oil mister:</strong> creates a very thin coating. Check the appliance manual before using commercial aerosol sprays, as some manufacturers advise against certain propellants or products on non-stick coatings.</li>
<li><strong>Marinade:</strong> the EVOO already present in a marinade may be enough.</li>
</ul>
<p>The oil should mainly go <strong>on the food</strong>. Pouring oil into the base does not recreate deep frying and can simply create smoke and mess.</p>
<h2>Potatoes with EVOO in an air fryer</h2>
<p>Potatoes are a classic use. After cutting, you can rinse them when the recipe benefits from removing surface starch, but dry them extremely well afterwards. Wet surfaces encourage steaming and make browning harder.</p>
<p>Toss with a small amount of EVOO, avoid packing the basket too tightly and shake or turn the potatoes partway through cooking. The thin oil film assists surface heat transfer and browning. The potatoes do not need to look drenched.</p>
<h2>Vegetables: when oil makes a real difference</h2>
<p>Broccoli, cauliflower, courgette, aubergine, peppers and onions all work in an air fryer. A little EVOO helps edges brown and carries herbs and spices across the surface. With high-moisture vegetables, avoid overcrowding because trapped steam produces a softer, more steamed result.</p>
<p>Aubergine can absorb a great deal of oil when heavily coated, so brushing or misting a thin layer is particularly useful.</p>
<h2>Meat and burgers: do they need oil?</h2>
<p>It depends on the cut. A burger or naturally fatty piece may need no added oil. A lean steak can benefit from a very light coating to encourage surface browning and help seasonings adhere. Oil does not compensate for excessive cooking time: an overcooked lean cut will still dry out.</p>
<p>EVOO also contributes flavour. That can complement strongly flavoured beef, while a more delicate dish may call for less oil or a milder EVOO.</p>
<h2>Does heating destroy EVOO's properties?</h2>
<p>Every oil changes with heat. Some aroma compounds and antioxidants gradually decrease as temperature, time and oxygen exposure increase. That does not mean EVOO instantly becomes unsuitable when heated.</p>
<p>Its oleic-acid-rich composition provides good heat stability. The sensible domestic approach is to use good oil, avoid unnecessary maximum heat and avoid prolonged heating with no food present.</p>
<h2>EVOO, regular olive oil or sunflower oil?</h2>
<p>All can be used in different contexts, but they are not identical. EVOO contributes flavour, natural antioxidants and good stability. Refined olive oil is more neutral. Conventional sunflower oil contains more polyunsaturated fat, while high-oleic sunflower oil has a different, more heat-stable fatty-acid profile.</p>
<p>For the broader comparison, see <a href="/en/olive-oil-or-sunflower-oil-for-frying-which-to-choose/">olive oil versus sunflower oil for frying</a>.</p>
<h2>Common mistakes with EVOO in an air fryer</h2>
<ul>
<li><strong>Using too much oil:</strong> it does not necessarily make food crisper and may simply collect underneath.</li>
<li><strong>Always selecting 200°C:</strong> many foods need lower heat and slightly more time.</li>
<li><strong>Failing to dry food:</strong> surface water creates steam and limits browning.</li>
<li><strong>Overfilling the basket:</strong> hot air needs space to circulate.</li>
<li><strong>Never turning or shaking:</strong> some foods brown unevenly if left untouched.</li>
<li><strong>Leaving old residues:</strong> accumulated fat and food can smoke during later uses.</li>
</ul>
<h2>Can you reuse oil from an air fryer?</h2>
<p>Usually there is very little free oil because most is applied to the food, while drippings in the tray become mixed with meat fat, water, seasoning and browned particles. That liquid is not equivalent to a pan of relatively clean frying oil. <strong>It generally makes little sense to save it as reusable frying oil.</strong></p>
<h2>The simple rule</h2>
<p>Yes, you can use EVOO in an air fryer. Use <strong>a small amount, distribute it evenly, start with relatively dry food and choose the temperature the recipe genuinely needs</strong>. This combines strong convection browning with the flavour and culinary qualities of extra virgin olive oil without immersing the food.</p>
